 # 8\. Your PostgreSQL table

 Your existing table works:

```
CREATE TABLE city
(
    code serial NOT NULL,
    ct_nm character varying(200)
);
```

 I would make one small improvement: make `code` the primary key and prevent duplicate city names.

```
ALTER TABLE city
ADD CONSTRAINT pk_city PRIMARY KEY (code);

ALTER TABLE city
ADD CONSTRAINT uq_city_ct_nm UNIQUE (ct_nm);
```

 Then your table becomes logically:

```
city
-----------------------
code     integer
ct_nm    varchar(200)
-----------------------
1        London
2        Paris
3        New York
```

 The `code` is automatically generated by PostgreSQL when you insert:

```
INSERT INTO city (ct_nm)
VALUES ('London');
```

 You don't need to provide `code` from your WinForms application.

---

 # 9\. Important: case-insensitive duplicates

 Our C# code checks:

```
WHERE UPPER(ct_nm) = UPPER(@cityName)
```

 So:

```
London
london
LONDON
LoNdOn
```

 are treated as the same city by the application.

 If you want PostgreSQL itself to enforce this rule, you can use a unique index:

```
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX uq_city_ct_nm_lower
ON city (LOWER(ct_nm));
```

 Then even if another application inserts `london`, PostgreSQL will prevent a duplicate `London`.

